  "query_preview": "..issued at 142AM MST.\nHigh pressure is in place over the Southwest and\nwill remain over the region for much of the forecast period. At\nthe surface an easterly pressure gradient is still in place and\nwill lead to breezy easterly winds through Monday. Expect speeds\n15-25 mph with gusts 25-35 mph. Isolated areas such as southern\nCochise County, the Catalina Mountains, and the Gila River\nValley could see gusts 40-50 mph, especially Sunday which is \nwhen winds are expected to be strongest. High temperatures will \nbe around normal to just below normal with low temperatures \nstaying elevated thanks to the downslope easterly winds. \n\nMonday models show a modest increase in moisture just to the\nsouth in Sonora from Hurricane Sarah, which will be a few \nhundred miles west of Cabo. PWATs may increase to 1.5\" in far \nwestern Pima County, but at this time NBM Pops are below 10 \npercent. This time of year with no synoptic scale influence \nsurface heating is not enough to form thunderstorms most of the\ntime. As a result rain chances stay south of the border with\nsome buildups possible over the higher terrain. With the ridge \nin place temperatures warm to a few degrees above normal Tuesday\ninto the end of next week. Also can expect another round of \nbreezy easterly winds starting next week Thursday.",
